(>> 4.1 Foundations of Data Communications and Transmission) Tj
T*
/F1 9 Tf
() Tj
T*
(Data communication is the exchange of data between two devices via some form of) Tj
T*
(transmission medium:) Tj
T*
(* Five Core Components: Message \(data/information\), Sender \(transmitter\), Receiver) Tj
T*
(\(sink\), Transmission Medium \(physical path\), Protocol \(governing set of rules\).) Tj
T*
(* Data Flow Directionality:) Tj
T*
(- Simplex: Unidirectional transmission \(e.g., traditional broadcast TV, keyboard to) Tj
T*
(CPU\).) Tj
T*
(- Half-Duplex: Bidirectional transmission, but only one direction at a time \(e.g.,) Tj
T*
(Walkie-talkie\).) Tj
T*
(- Full-Duplex: Simultaneous bidirectional transmission \(e.g., modern telephone system,) Tj
T*
(switched Ethernet\).) Tj
T*
(* Network Geographical Scales: Personal Area Network \(PAN - Bluetooth ~10 m\), Local) Tj
T*
(Area Network \(LAN - office building ~1 km\), Metropolitan Area Network \(MAN - smart) Tj
T*
(city ~50 km\), Wide Area Network \(WAN - continent/globe\).) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
/F2 9.5 Tf
(>> 4.2 Physical Network Topologies) Tj
T*
/F1 9 Tf
() Tj
T*
(* 1. Mesh Topology: Every node connected to every other node via dedicated) Tj
T*
(point-to-point links. Links required for N nodes: L = N\(N - 1\) / 2; I/O ports per) Tj
T*
(node = N - 1. Advantages: High fault tolerance, robust privacy. Drawback: Massive) Tj
T*
(cabling cost.) Tj
T*
(* 2. Star Topology: Each device connected to a central hub/switch via point-to-point) Tj
T*
(link. Highly popular \(standard Ethernet LAN\). Single cable fault does not affect) Tj
T*
(network; central switch failure halts network.) Tj
T*
(* 3. Bus Topology: Single shared backbone cable \(multipoint\) terminated with) Tj
T*
(resistors. Inexpensive; difficult fault isolation; collision prone.) Tj
T*
(* 4. Ring Topology: Nodes connected in closed loop; tokens circulate) Tj
T*
(unidirectionally. Deterministic delay; single break disrupts entire ring unless) Tj
T*
(dual-ring \(FDDI\) is used.) Tj
T*
(* 5. Hybrid Topology: Combination of two or more topologies \(e.g., Star-Bus or Tree) Tj
T*
